---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import data from '../data/pipeline.json';

const { etaHorizon: h, rules } = data;
const n = (v: number, d = 0) =>
  v.toLocaleString('en-GB', { minimumFractionDigits: d, maximumFractionDigits: d });

const r13 = rules.find((r) => r.ruleId === 'R13');

// Everything up to a year. Beyond that the rollover cannot reach, so a bin there would be a
// different phenomenon sharing an axis.
const MAX_DAYS = 366;
const bins = h.bins.filter((b) => b.daysAhead <= MAX_DAYS);
const peak = Math.max(...bins.map((b) => b.fixes));

// Square root rather than log. A log axis makes an empty bin and a bin holding five fixes look
// alike at this scale, and the point of the chart is that one of them is empty.
const W = 960;
const H = 260;
const x = (day: number) => (day / MAX_DAYS) * W;
const y = (fixes: number) => H - (Math.sqrt(fixes) / Math.sqrt(peak)) * H;
const barWidth = Math.max(1.1, W / MAX_DAYS);

const threshold = h.bandContainingTheThreshold;
const straggler = bins
  .filter((b) => b.fixes > 0 && b.daysAhead > 33 && b.daysAhead < 131)
  .map((b) => `day ${b.daysAhead} carries ${n(b.fixes)}`);

const nearTerm = bins.filter((b) => b.daysAhead <= 33).reduce((t, b) => t + b.fixes, 0);
const desert = bins
  .filter((b) => b.daysAhead >= 34 && b.daysAhead <= 130)
  .reduce((t, b) => t + b.fixes, 0);
---
<Base
  title="The ETA cliff — a field that cannot expire, so it rolls over instead"
  description="The AIS estimated-time-of-arrival field carries no year. A forgotten ETA is never left behind in the past; it is catapulted up to a year into the future. You can see the seam."
>
  <h1>The field that cannot expire</h1>

  <p class="lede prose">
    Every AIS message can carry an estimated time of arrival, typed in by the crew. It has{' '}
    <strong>no year</strong> — month, day, hour and minute, and nothing else. So a decoder meeting
    a date that has already gone by has no way to read it as the past, and must assume the next
    one. A forgotten ETA is never left behind. It is rolled forward, and reappears months ahead.
  </p>

  <p class="prose">
    Here is every ETA in the feed, placed by how far ahead of its own message it pointed.
  </p>

  <figure class="chart">
    <svg viewBox={`0 0 ${W} ${H + 42}`} role="img"
         aria-label={`Histogram of AIS ETAs by days ahead. Fixes fall away smoothly to about day 33, then a ${threshold?.days}-day stretch carries none at all, before the count climbs again and peaks near one year.`}>
      {threshold && (
        <rect class="band" x={x(threshold.fromDays)} y="0"
              width={x(threshold.toDays + 1) - x(threshold.fromDays)} height={H} />
      )}

      {bins.map((b) => (
        b.fixes > 0 && (
          <rect class={b.daysAhead > h.flaggedBeyondDays ? 'col stale' : 'col'}
                x={x(b.daysAhead)} y={y(b.fixes)}
                width={barWidth} height={H - y(b.fixes)}>
            <title>{`day ${b.daysAhead}: ${n(b.fixes)} fixes`}</title>
          </rect>
        )
      ))}

      <line class="axis" x1="0" y1={H} x2={W} y2={H} />

      {[0, 30, 60, 90, 180, 270, 365].map((d) => (
        <>
          <line class="tick" x1={x(d)} y1={H} x2={x(d)} y2={H + 5} />
          <text class="label" x={x(d)} y={H + 20}>{d}</text>
        </>
      ))}
      <text class="label axis-title" x={W / 2} y={H + 38}>days ahead of the message that carried it</text>
    </svg>
    <figcaption class="note">
      Bar height is the square root of the count, so a day carrying five fixes is still visible
      beside one carrying two million. A day carrying none has no bar at all — which is the point.
      The shaded stretch is the {threshold?.days} days where R13 draws its line.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <ul class="stats">
    <li class="stat"><b>{n(h.fixesWithAnEta)}</b><span>fixes carrying an ETA</span></li>
    <li class="stat ok"><b>{n(nearTerm)}</b><span>pointing inside 33 days</span></li>
    <li class="stat alarm"><b>{n(desert)}</b><span>across the next 97 days</span></li>
    <li class="stat alarm"><b>{n(r13?.flagged ?? 0)}</b><span>flagged stale by R13</span></li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The seam</h2>

  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      Real voyage plans behave the way you would expect: most ETAs point at today or tomorrow, and
      the count falls away smoothly for about a month. Then it stops. Between day 34 and day 130 —{' '}
      <strong>97 days</strong> — the entire feed carries {n(desert)} fixes, against tens of
      thousands a day on either side. {straggler.length > 0 && (
        <>Only {straggler.join(' and ')}.</>
      )}
    </p>
    <p>
      After that the count climbs back and piles up towards a year out. Those are not voyage plans.
      A ship does not know in September where it will be next August. They are dates nobody has
      touched since they passed, rolled forward by a decoder that had no year to tell it otherwise.
    </p>
  </div>

  <blockquote class="prose">
    <p>
      This is what makes the ETA different from the other hand-typed fields. A stale navigational
      status looks like a plausible status. A stale ETA cannot look like a plausible ETA — the
      encoding will not let it sit in the past — so it has to announce itself, months out, where
      nobody would ever have typed it.
    </p>
  </blockquote>

  <h2>Why the threshold cannot matter</h2>

  <p class="prose">
    Rule <strong>R13</strong> flags an ETA more than <strong>{h.flaggedBeyondDays} days</strong>{' '}
    ahead as stale, and flags {n(r13?.flagged ?? 0)} fixes — about one in fifteen of everything
    stored. A threshold like that usually deserves an argument about whether it is set in the right
    place.
    {threshold
      ? <> Not here: <strong>day {h.flaggedBeyondDays} sits inside a run of {threshold.days} days
        ({threshold.fromDays}–{threshold.toDays}) on which no fix lands at all.</strong> Anywhere in
        that run classifies every message identically, so the exact value cannot change a single
        answer.</>
      : <> On this window no such run exists, which means the threshold <em>is</em> deciding
        something and deserves the argument after all.</>}
  </p>

  <p class="note prose">
    That claim is recomputed from the data every time this page is built, rather than quoted from
    the decision that made it. A threshold justified by a gap that has since closed is a threshold
    nobody is justifying.
  </p>

  <h2>What is not being claimed</h2>

  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      Nothing is rejected for this. A stale ETA is still exactly what the vessel broadcast, and the
      pipeline keeps it and records the doubt beside it — the same treatment every hand-typed field
      gets here. {n(h.fixesWithAPastEta)} fixes even carry an ETA that had already passed, which
      the rollover is supposed to make impossible; they are kept too.
    </p>
    <p>
      And it is not a claim about any crew. An ETA is a forecast, and a forecast nobody revised is
      the most ordinary thing on a ship. What the shape shows is that the <em>field</em> has no way
      to say "I am out of date" — so it says something much louder instead.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
